isomd5sum provides a way of making use of the ISO9660 application data
area to store md5sum data about the iso.  This allows you to check the
iso given nothing more than the iso itself.

In addition to the md5sum, the serial number of an X.509 certificate can
optionally be embedded into the same application data area.  This is useful
for recording which certificate an image is associated with.  The serial is
stored exactly as reported by

  openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -serial
  serial=21B3CA436F1D60287FDA31DA91F5F697

To implant both the md5sum and a certificate serial number into an iso::

  implantisomd5 --cert /etc/pki/tls/certs/treasury-cert.pem image.iso

The certificate may be in PEM or DER format.  The embedded serial can be
read back together with the md5sum information::

  checkisomd5 --md5sumonly image.iso

Embedding a certificate serial does not affect md5 verification: the whole
application data area is excluded from the checksum, so "checkisomd5" still
passes.  Building isomd5sum requires OpenSSL (libcrypto) for this feature.

Cryptographic signing
----------------------
The embedded md5sum can additionally be signed so that a consumer can prove
the image's checksum was produced by the holder of a certificate's private
key.  The signature is written to a detached file "<iso>.sig" (base64) next
to the image, which keeps it independent of the limited application data
area and works with RSA, ECDSA and Ed25519 keys.

To sign while implanting::

  implantisomd5 --cert cert.pem --sign-key key.pem image.iso
  # writes image.iso and image.iso.sig

To verify the signature against the certificate's public key::

  checkisomd5 --cert cert.pem image.iso

"checkisomd5" first performs the normal md5 media check and then reports the
signature result as "Signature status: VALID" or "INVALID".  A failed or
missing signature yields a non-zero exit status.  Note that the md5 check and
the signature check are complementary: the md5 check detects tampering with
the image content, while the signature proves the embedded md5 was authorized
by the certificate holder.

Distributing a signed image
---------------------------
Verification requires THREE things, which must be provided together:

  1. the image            e.g.  rear.iso
  2. the detached sig     e.g.  rear.iso.sig   (same directory, same base name)
  3. the certificate      e.g.  rear-cert.pem  (public; safe to distribute)

The signature file must sit next to the image with the exact name
"<iso>.sig"; if it is moved away or renamed, "checkisomd5 --cert" reports
"Signature status: MISSING".  The private key used for signing stays on the
signing host and is NEVER needed (or shipped) for verification.

Note: "checkisomd5 --cert" trusts the public key inside the given
certificate; it does not validate the certificate's trust chain, expiry or
revocation.  Validate the certificate itself separately if required, e.g.
"openssl verify -CAfile ca-bundle.pem rear-cert.pem".

isomd5sum is hosted on GitHub. The source can be
grabbed via git with
  git clone https://github.com/rhinstaller/isomd5sum.git
or browsed via
  https://github.com/rhinstaller/isomd5sum

Releases of isomd5sum are automatically generated by the tags. eg. for the
1.0.12 release::
  https://github.com/rhinstaller/isomd5sum/archive/1.0.12.tar.gz

You can send questions, enhancements, etc to anaconda-devel-list@redhat.com.
